{"step_index":851,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:23Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:23Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 75\n1: ---\n2: name: fuse\n3: description: Build, debug, and review Linux FUSE filesystems with the official libfuse userspace library. Use when implementing a FUSE filesystem, choosing between libfuse high-level vs low-level APIs, wiring callbacks, building with fuse3/pkg-config, mounting for local testing, or troubleshooting fusermount3 and permission issues.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # FUSE\n7: \n8: Use the official `libfuse/libfuse` project as the baseline for Linux FUSE work.\n9: \n10: ## Quick Start\n11: \n12: 1. Confirm the task is Linux FUSE userspace work, not kernel module work.\n13: 2. Prefer the official `libfuse` interfaces and examples before suggesting wrappers.\n14: 3. Decide API style:\n15: - Use the high-level API for simpler path-based filesystems.\n16: - Use the low-level API for inode-level control, explicit replies, or advanced behavior.\n17: 4. Build with `pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs` unless the project already uses another build system.\n18: 5. For local runs, prefer foreground debug mode first: `-f -d`.\n19: \n20: ## Workflow\n21: \n22: ### 1. Choose the API\n23: \n24: Use the high-level API when the user needs a straightforward filesystem and path callbacks are sufficient.\n25: Read `include/fuse.h` semantics first.\n26: \n27: Use the low-level API when the user needs inode handling, async-style explicit replies, or finer control over request lifetime.\n28: Read `include/fuse_lowlevel.h` semantics first.\n29: \n30: ### 2. Start from official examples\n31: \n32: Check the `example/` directory in `libfuse/libfuse` before drafting handlers from scratch.\n33: For passthrough, mirror, hello-world, or minimal callback wiring, adapt an official example instead of inventing a new skeleton.\n34: \n35: ### 3. Build correctly\n36: \n37: Use these defaults unless the project already defines them:\n38: \n39: ```bash\n40: pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs\n41: ```\n42: \n43: Typical manual build pattern:\n44: \n45: ```bash\n46: gcc fs.c -o fs $(pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs)\n47: ```\n48: \n49: If the project vendors `libfuse`, follow its existing Meson/Ninja setup instead of replacing the build flow.\n50: "}
{"step_index":852,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 126\n1: ---\n2: name: btrfs\n3: description: >\n4:   Btrfs filesystem administration: subvolumes, snapshots, rollback, balance,\n5:   scrub, RAID profiles, compression, quotas, send/receive backups, and\n6:   troubleshooting.\n7:   MUST consult when installing, configuring, or troubleshooting btrfs.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"btrfs\"\n10:   - \"Btrfs\"\n11:   - \"btrfs snapshot\"\n12:   - \"btrfs subvolume\"\n13:   - \"btrfs balance\"\n14:   - \"btrfs scrub\"\n15:   - \"copy-on-write filesystem\"\n16:   - \"CoW filesystem\"\n17:   - \"btrfs-progs\"\n18:   - \"mkfs.btrfs\"\n19:   - \"snapper\"\n20:   - \"btrbk\"\n21: globs:\n22:   - \"**/fstab\"\n23:   - \"**/etc/fstab\"\n24: last_verified: \"unverified\"\n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## Identity\n28: \n29: | Property | Value |\n30: |----------|-------|\n31: | **Type** | Kernel built-in filesystem (no loadable module needed on most distros) |\n32: | **CLI tool** | `btrfs` (subcommands: `filesystem`, `subvolume`, `balance`, `scrub`, `device`, `quota`, `qgroup`, `check`, `defragment`, `rescue`) |\n33: | **Distro install** | `apt install btrfs-progs` / `dnf install btrfs-progs` |\n34: | **Create** | `mkfs.btrfs` |\n35: | **Kernel version matters** | Significant stability improvements landed between 4.x and 6.x; older kernels have known bugs with RAID-5/6 and balance |\n36: \n37: ## Quick Start\n38: \n39: ```bash\n40: sudo apt install btrfs-progs\n41: sudo mkfs.btrfs -L mydata /dev/sdX1\n42: sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/mydata\n43: btrfs filesystem show /mnt/mydata\n44: btrfs device stats /mnt/mydata\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ## Key Operations\n48: \n49: | Task | Command |\n50: |------|---------|"}
{"step_index":853,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 213\n1: ---\n2: name: incus\n3: description: >\n4:   Incus system containers and VMs: instance/profile YAML, disk devices,\n5:   native storage pools and volumes, Btrfs subvolumes, lifecycle and isolation.\n6:   Consult before creating or changing an Incus instance, profile, disk,\n7:   storage volume, root binding or provisioning helper.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"Incus\"\n10:   - \"Incus disk\"\n11:   - \"Incus profile\"\n12:   - \"Incus storage\"\n13:   - \"container root disk\"\n14:   - \"native storage volume\"\n15: last_verified: \"2026-10-19\"\n16: ---\n17: \n18: # Incus: configuration first, storage-driver ownership\n19: \n20: Project-local skill authored from the official Incus documentation below;\n21: not an imported third-party skill or a container-sandbox wrapper. Documentation\n22: review is not proof that any example has been applied to this host. Check the\n23: installed client/server version and applicable API extensions before using\n24: version-dependent options.\n25: \n26: ## Start with the actual declaration\n27: \n28: An Incus instance is configured through instance properties, `config`,\n29: `devices` and ordered `profiles`. The CLI exposes this as YAML; do not invent\n30: a separate authoritative YAML file on disk. Incus owns its configuration.\n31: \n32: Read both local and expanded instance configuration. Later profiles override\n33: earlier ones; instance-local configuration overrides profiles. Editing a\n34: shared profile affects every instance that uses it. Do not use expanded\n35: configuration as a blind replacement: it flattens inherited settings.\n36: \n37: Read-only infrastructure diagnostics, within the assigned maintenance scope:\n38: \n39: ```sh\n40: incus version\n41: incus config show <instance>\n42: incus config show <instance> --expanded\n43: incus profile show <profile>\n44: incus storage show <pool>\n45: incus storage volume list <pool>\n46: incus storage volume show <pool> container/<instance>\n47: ```\n48: \n49: Confirm the selected remote, project, instance type, native UUID and stopped\n50: or running state. A name alone is not an identity or authorization proof."}
{"step_index":854,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:25Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 352\n1: ---\n2: name: rust-memory-optimization\n3: description: |\n4:   Memory optimization and allocation reduction in Rust. Use when:\n5:   - User is designing memory-sensitive or low-latency systems\n6:   - Optimizing data structures to reduce memory footprint\n7:   - Replacing heap allocations with stack-allocated/bounded structures (SmallVec, ArrayVec, ThinVec)\n8:   - Handling small strings with optimizations (CompactString)\n9:   - Reusing collections via clear() in loops\n10:   - Implementing zero-copy decoding and references\n11:   - Structuring arena-allocated memory (Bumpalo)\n12:   - Measuring memory usage, profiling heap, or diagnosing leaks\n13: ---\n14: \n15: \n16: \n17: ## Quick Navigation\n18: \n19: - [references/stack_allocations.md](references/stack_allocations.md)\n20: - [references/arenas.md](references/arenas.md)\n21: \n22: # Rust Memory Optimization Guide\n23: \n24: Advanced strategies for reducing allocations, shrinking layout sizes, and boosting performance.\n25: \n26: ## Core Rules & Patterns\n27: \n28: ### 1. Bounded Heap-Free Collections\n29: For collections that are usually small, avoid standard vectors to bypass heap allocations.\n30: - **`SmallVec`**: Stack-allocated up to a threshold, overflows to the heap.\n31: - **`ArrayVec`**: Fixed-capacity array wrapper; never allocates on the heap.\n32: - **`ThinVec`**: Only takes a single pointer size when empty, reducing size in enum layouts.\n33: \n34: ```rust\n35: use smallvec::{smallvec, SmallVec};\n36: use arrayvec::ArrayVec;\n37: \n38: // SmallVec: Allocates 4 items on the stack. Goes to heap only if count > 4.\n39: let mut local_tags: SmallVec<[String; 4]> = smallvec![];\n40: local_tags.push(\"Rust\".to_string());\n41: \n42: // ArrayVec: Absolute limit of 8 items on the stack. No heap overflow.\n43: let mut coordinates: ArrayVec<f64, 8> = ArrayVec::new();\n44: coordinates.push(42.0);\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ### 2. Small String Optimization (SSO)\n48: Standard `String` consumes 24 bytes and allocates heap space immediately. Use `CompactString` for inline small strings (under 24 bytes) without allocating.\n49: \n50: ```rust"}
{"step_index":855,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:24Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:25Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 76\n1: ---\n2: name: memory-safety-patterns\n3: description: Implement memory-safe programming with RAII, ownership, smart pointers, and resource management across Rust, C++, and C. Use when writing safe systems code, managing resources, or preventing memory bugs.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Memory Safety Patterns\n7: \n8: Cross-language patterns for memory-safe programming including RAII, ownership, smart pointers, and resource management.\n9: \n10: ## When to Use This Skill\n11: \n12: - Writing memory-safe systems code\n13: - Managing resources (files, sockets, memory)\n14: - Preventing use-after-free and leaks\n15: - Implementing RAII patterns\n16: - Choosing between languages for safety\n17: - Debugging memory issues\n18: \n19: ## Core Concepts\n20: \n21: ### 1. Memory Bug Categories\n22: \n23: | Bug Type             | Description                      | Prevention        |\n24: | -------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n25: | **Use-after-free**   | Access freed memory              | Ownership, RAII   |\n26: | **Double-free**      | Free same memory twice           | Smart pointers    |\n27: | **Memory leak**      | Never free memory                | RAII, GC          |\n28: | **Buffer overflow**  | Write past buffer end            | Bounds checking   |\n29: | **Dangling pointer** | Pointer to freed memory          | Lifetime tracking |\n30: | **Data race**        | Concurrent unsynchronized access | Ownership, Sync   |\n31: \n32: ### 2. Safety Spectrum\n33: \n34: ```\n35: Manual (C) → Smart Pointers (C++) → Ownership (Rust) → GC (Go, Java)\n36: Less safe                                              More safe\n37: More control                                           Less control\n38: ```\n39: \n40: ## Detailed patterns and worked examples\n41: \n42: Detailed pattern documentation lives in `references/details.md`. Read that file when the navigation tier above is insufficient.\n43: \n44: ## Best Practices\n45: \n46: ### Do's\n47: \n48: - **Prefer RAII** - Tie resource lifetime to scope\n49: - **Use smart pointers** - Avoid raw pointers in C++\n50: - **Understand ownership** - Know who owns what"}
